Well, it was a bit more significant than that, and
recognized as such at the time: he proved that the
emphasis on _score_ as a measure of game "worth" was
misplaced, since even a PC killed by a newt could
run up any level of score you care to name, and in
this case, one past the highest score the game could
correctly represent. The intent, and I think an
intent achieved, was to turn people's minds to new
ways to measure quality of play in NetHack, ways
used in the 2004 tournament.

> * One possible solution to the end game rider
> problem,

There is no "end game rider problem"; one can as
easily reverse genocide trolls a few times and do
pretty much the same thing, and there are dozens of
other ways of achieving endless score improvements.

    All using one of the riders did was speed up the
    process, but one could probably wrap the
    "expect()" program cleverly around a
    text-mode-NetHack pudding farming session, and
    do the equivalent _automatically_, if running up
    the score remained important to you. The point
    made is that high score, in isolation from
    other, better ways of measuring game play
    prowess, should _not_ remain inportant to you.

What there is, is a "'score is everything' mentality
problem", that both emphasises high score to the
exclusion of other, better measures of merit, and
also sends folks like Damerell into frothing fits
about stuff like pudding farming, altar squatting,
or credit cloning that happen to run up the score as
an unimportant and unintended side effect of the
real use to which the patience-mode player is
putting them, and which thus devalue the (already
proved valueless by eit-brian) emphasis those
players put on the scores in the high score table.

All the attempts to "fix" the scoring achieve is to
divert attention from the place the real "fix" is
needed, to the mental health of those fixated on
"winning" to the exclusion of others playing the
game purely for the fun of it.

